#!/usr/bin/env ruby
# frozen_string_literal: true

require "fileutils"
require "json"
require "open3"
require "pathname"
require "tmpdir"

# What a green boot, a green suite and a clean `rubocop` all agree on, and all
# three are wrong about: which constants in `lib/` resolve only because the
# loader happens to reach their defining file before anything names them.
#
# `spec/zeitwerk_spec.rb` asks the loader whether it can FIND each constant
# `lib/` defines, and the answer is yes for hundreds of them that have no path
# of their own -- findable through the file, never through the name. That spec
# owns the orphan invariant and this script does not restate it. The question
# here is the next one down, and it is the one a rename answers badly: does the
# tree still boot when the files arrive in a different order?
#
# Four modes:
#
#   census  (default)   every finding, in full, behind its own banner
#   --counts            the summary lines only
#   --check             the RATCHET: non-zero when a finding is not in {Known}
#
# == How the question is asked
#
# By asking it, not by reading for it. A static scan cannot tell a load-time
# constant reference from one inside a method body that will never run at boot,
# and that distinction IS the finding -- so the tree is really booted, in a
# child, four times:
#
#   1. SURVEY. `require "lain"` the normal way and walk {Lain} with
#      `const_source_location`, which is Ruby's own answer to "what file defines
#      this constant" rather than a regex's. The same walk copies `lib/` to a
#      scratch tree with every `require_relative` line removed -- nothing to
#      remove today, and kept because the strip is what makes the claim "the
#      loader alone did this" true of whatever the tree later becomes.
#   2/3/4. PROBE, three times, over the scratch tree: register Zeitwerk's
#      autoloads, then reach every managed constant in sorted order, then in
#      REVERSE sorted order, then in a SHUFFLED one. Order matters because
#      Zeitwerk eager loads a directory sorted, so a reference that resolves
#      today may be resolving by ALPHABETICAL LUCK -- `epic/document.rb` simply
#      sorts before `epic/intake.rb`. Reversing turns that luck into a finding;
#      run forward only and {Lain::Epic::MalformedDocument} is invisible.
#
#      The third order is there because two are a sample, not a proof: an
#      `ErrorMiddleware` reference survived both fixed orders and fell over in
#      5 of 12 shuffles, because something further along in reverse happened to
#      load its defining file first. The seed is printed on every run, green
#      included, so any sample can be replayed.
#
# The probe installs a `Module#const_missing` that requires the defining file
# and records the site, so ONE run reports every remaining reference instead of
# stopping at the first. It is PREPENDED rather than defined: `super` has to
# reach Ruby's own `const_missing` so that a name this census knows nothing
# about still raises the NameError it should, un-masked. A name it does know is
# recorded once and then resolved, because the run has to continue to be a
# census at all.
#
# == What it cannot see
#
#   * A reference recorded ONCE. After the first hit the defining file is
#     loaded, so a second file naming the same constant resolves silently. The
#     CONSTANT set is complete across the two orders; the SITE list names one
#     site per constant per order, not all of them.
#   * A load-time reference reached only through a branch, an `if defined?`, or
#     a platform this box is not.
#   * Anything `spec/`, `exe/` or the nvim plugin names. This boots `lib/`.
#   * Anything the loader does not manage. The probe drives its own
#     `all_expected_cpaths`, so a path the loader was told to ignore is never
#     probed. That list is empty today, which is what makes a clean census a
#     claim about all of `lib/` rather than about part of it.
#
# Unlike `bin/comment-census` and `bin/spec-census` this is NOT pure stdlib in
# effect: the children need Bundler, the gems and the compiled extension,
# because a boot is the only thing that can answer the question. The PARENT --
# everything below that classifies, ratchets and formats -- touches none of
# that, which is what lets `spec/lain/zeitwerk_census_spec.rb` exercise it
# against fixtures.
module ZeitwerkCensus
  REPO_ROOT = Pathname(__dir__).join("..").expand_path

  # The seeded ratchet, and the reason it is a list of NAMES rather than
  # `bin/spec-census`'s count. That script's own doc explains why 184 cannot be
  # an allowlist: one sized to the count is a disabled guard wearing a gate's
  # name. The inverse argument applies at this size. Each entry below is a
  # single latent boot failure with an address, a fix, and no siblings to hide
  # among -- so identity is the stronger ratchet: a count lets a new orphan in
  # free the moment somebody fixes an old one, which is exactly the trade a
  # rename makes.
  #
  # An entry is REMOVED when its finding is fixed, never edited to point
  # somewhere else. A new entry is a commit that says why, the way a raised
  # `.rubocop.yml` limit is.
  module Known
    # A file the loader expects to define a constant, that defines nothing once
    # its `require_relative` lines are gone: the unit index is the whole file.
    # The fix is to give the file the `module Foo` it already stands for.
    NAMESPACES = [].freeze

    # A constant reached while a file LOADS whose own name maps to no file --
    # written in a class body, or in a method default that a load-time constant
    # assignment then calls. The fix is to move it to the path its name
    # implies, or to stop reaching it at load time.
    REFERENCES = [].freeze
  end

  # One thing that stops resolving in some load order, in the terms a reader
  # fixes it in. `name` is the ratchet key and is deliberately NOT a
  # line number: a site drifts within days, and CLAUDE.md's rule for recording
  # a flaky spec by name rather than by line is the same rule for the same
  # reason. `where` carries the drifting detail, for reading only.
  #
  # `blame` is the one extra fact each shape carries so {Cascades} can ask
  # whether this finding is already counted above: for a `:namespace` it is the
  # constant path that file fails to define, for an `:unclassified` it is the
  # file that really defines the constant the failure named, and for a
  # `:reference` it is nil. One field rather than three because exactly one
  # question is asked of it.
  Finding = Struct.new(:shape, :name, :where, :blame) do
    def to_s = "#{name.ljust(38)} #{where}"
  end

  # The four shapes. Two carry an allowlist and ratchet against it; `:cascade`
  # is reported and never ratcheted, because each of its entries is already
  # counted once as the namespace it falls behind and a reader who fixes that
  # namespace fixes these for free. `:unclassified` ratchets against an EMPTY
  # allowlist, so any probe failure this script cannot place fails `--check` --
  # `bin/comment-census` sets that house precedent: teach the classifier
  # before you sweep.
  SHAPES = {
    namespace: { title: "index-only namespace -- the file defines nothing without its requires",
                 known: Known::NAMESPACES, ratchet: true },
    reference: { title: "load-time orphan reference -- reached while a file LOADS, no path maps to it",
                 known: Known::REFERENCES, ratchet: true },
    cascade: { title: "already counted above -- fix the namespace it falls behind, not these",
               known: [], ratchet: false },
    unclassified: { title: "UNCLASSIFIED -- teach bin/zeitwerk-census before believing this run",
                    known: [], ratchet: true }
  }.freeze

  # Zeitwerk's own words for an index-only namespace. Parsed rather than
  # guessed at because the file that FAILED to define the constant is the
  # finding, while the file being required when it surfaced is a cascade --
  # `tools/ast_search.rb` fails for `structural.rb`'s reason, and reporting
  # ast_search.rb would send a reader to the wrong file.
  MISSING_CONSTANT = /\Aexpected file (?<file>\S+) to define constant (?<cpath>\S+), but didn't\z/

  # The namespace findings, as the two questions every later finding is checked
  # against. Both answers mean "already counted once, above, and fixed by the
  # same edit":
  #
  #   * it lives UNDER a flagged file's subtree -- nothing under
  #     `provider/http/` can load while `provider/http.rb` defines no
  #     `Lain::Provider::HTTP`, and the failures there surface as NameErrors
  #     against the `anthropic` gem's own top-level module, the constant
  #     shadowing CLAUDE.md already records, reached through a hole this census
  #     opened rather than a finding of its own.
  #   * it IS the constant a flagged file fails to define, seen from the other
  #     side -- `provider/ollama/transport.rb` naming `Lain::Provider::HTTP` at
  #     load time is one fact with `provider/http.rb`, not a second, and listing
  #     both has a reader budget one more fix than there is.
  #
  # Asked of `blame` rather than of the failing SITE, because a cascade surfaces
  # wherever the load happened to reach: the one that cost this census a red
  # gate arrived at `Lain::LOADER.eager_load`, which is not a file and is under
  # no subtree at all.
  class Cascades
    def initialize(findings)
      flagged = findings.select { _1.shape == :namespace }
      @subtrees = flagged.map { "#{_1.name.delete_suffix(".rb")}/" }
      @cpaths = flagged.map(&:blame)
    end

    def covers?(found)
      return @subtrees.any? { found.blame.to_s.start_with?(_1) } if found.shape == :unclassified
      return @cpaths.include?(found.name) if found.shape == :reference

      false
    end
  end

  # Turns two probe runs into one sorted, de-duplicated finding list. Its own
  # module so the classification can be exercised against fixture Hashes:
  # booting the tree is {Boot}'s job and is not a precondition for testing this.
  module Classify
    module_function

    # @param runs [Array<Hash>] one decoded probe result per load order
    # @return [Array<Finding>]
    def findings(runs)
      first_seen = (runs.flat_map { |run| references(run) } + runs.flat_map { |run| failures(run) })
                   .group_by(&:name).values.map(&:first)
      demote(first_seen).sort_by { |found| [found.shape.to_s, found.name] }
    end

    def demote(findings)
      cascades = Cascades.new(findings)
      findings.map do |found|
        cascades.covers?(found) ? Finding.new(:cascade, found.name, found.where, found.blame) : found
      end
    end

    # `from` is where the name is written and `at` is the load-time body that
    # reached it, and they are often different files: `Mode::LayerSet` is
    # written as a keyword-argument default in `mode.rb`, which runs only
    # because `cli/command/mode.rb` calls `Mode.new` while assigning a
    # constant. A reader needs the second to understand why this fires at all.
    def references(run)
      run.fetch("hits").map do |hit|
        Finding.new(:reference, hit.fetch("const"),
                    "#{hit.fetch("from")}#{" via #{hit["at"]}" if hit["at"]} " \
                    "-> #{hit.fetch("defined_in")} [#{run.fetch("order")}]")
      end
    end

    # A probe failure is an index-only namespace when Zeitwerk says so in as
    # many words, and unclassified otherwise. An unclassified one is NAMED by
    # the file that defines the constant it could not find, when the probe could
    # resolve one -- the site it surfaced at is the cascade, and naming that
    # would send a reader to `(eager_load)`.
    def failures(run)
      run.fetch("fails").map { |failure| failure(failure, run.fetch("order")) }
    end

    def failure(failure, order)
      named = MISSING_CONSTANT.match(failure.fetch("message"))
      return Finding.new(:namespace, named[:file], "#{named[:cpath]} [#{order}]", named[:cpath]) if named

      blame = failure["blamed"] || failure.fetch("file")
      Finding.new(:unclassified, blame,
                  "#{failure.fetch("class")}: #{failure.fetch("message")} at #{failure.fetch("file")} [#{order}]",
                  blame)
    end
  end

  # One shape's findings against one shape's allowlist: what is new, what the
  # allowlist still names, and what it names that is already fixed.
  Census = Struct.new(:shape, :title, :found, :known, :ratchet) do
    def names = found.map(&:name)

    def new_entries = found.reject { |entry| known.include?(entry.name) }

    def stale = known - names

    # A stale entry does NOT fail. Failing on good news makes the guard
    # adversarial to whoever just fixed something, and the entry is printed
    # loudly either way -- the same posture `bin/spec-census` takes when a
    # count lands under its ceiling.
    def over? = ratchet && new_entries.any?
  end

  # The three children, and the scratch tree they share. Everything here is
  # `spawn`/`JSON`; the code the children RUN lives in {Survey} and {Probe} in
  # this same file, reached through the flags {CLI} keeps out of its usage.
  module Boot
    # Two passes are a SAMPLE of the order space, not a proof over it, and the
    # tree has already had a finding in the gap between them: `ErrorMiddleware`
    # named six error classes while its class body ran and resolved in both
    # sorted orders alike -- forward because `error.rb` sorts first, reverse
    # because something further along happened to pull it in. A third pass,
    # shuffled, is what closes that gap, and one more boot costs ~2s.
    #
    # It asks the question DIFFERENTLY, and that difference is the point. The
    # sorted passes `require` each file by PATH, which is stricter than the
    # loader and partly unfaithful to it -- requiring a nested file before its
    # namespace file is something Zeitwerk itself never does, and reporting the
    # re-entrant requires that follow would be reporting the probe. The
    # shuffled pass resolves each expected constant by NAME instead, which is
    # exactly what an autoload does, so every failure it reports is one a real
    # caller could reach.
    ORDERS = %w[forward reverse].freeze

    # Printed with every run and honoured from the environment, because a
    # sampled guard whose failing sample cannot be replayed is a guard nobody
    # can act on: `ZEITWERK_CENSUS_SEED=<n> bin/zeitwerk-census` re-runs the
    # exact shuffle a report names.
    SEED_ENV = "ZEITWERK_CENSUS_SEED"

    module_function

    def seed = Integer(ENV.fetch(SEED_ENV, Random.new_seed % 1_000_000))

    # One decoded probe result per load order. The scratch tree is named
    # by `mktmpdir` rather than by a fixed path: CLAUDE.md records a generic
    # filename in a shared scratchpad as shared mutable state between agents,
    # and the failure mode is a confident green from another tree.
    #
    # @param shuffle_seed [Integer] the seed the third pass shuffles by
    # @return [Array<Hash>]
    def runs(shuffle_seed = seed)
      Dir.mktmpdir("zeitwerk-census-") do |scratch|
        spec = File.join(scratch, "survey.json")
        child("--survey", spec, File.join(scratch, "lib"))
        (ORDERS + ["shuffled:#{shuffle_seed}"]).map { |order| JSON.parse(child("--probe", spec, order)) }
      end
    end

    # Bundler and the compiled extension both have to be there, and a child
    # that half-boots reports a clean census of nothing -- so its stderr is
    # carried into the raise rather than dropped.
    def child(*args)
      out, err, status = Open3.capture3(*command, __FILE__, *args)
      return out if status.success?

      raise "#{File.basename(__FILE__)} #{args.first} failed (#{status.exitstatus})\n#{err}"
    end

    # `bundle exec` unless already inside it, because the children need the
    # gems and this script is also useful typed bare. The REAL `lib/` is on the
    # child's load path either way: the survey requires lain through it, and the
    # probe reaches the compiled extension through it while Zeitwerk drives the
    # scratch tree by absolute path.
    def command
      inner = [RbConfig.ruby, "-I#{REPO_ROOT.join("lib")}"]
      ENV["BUNDLE_GEMFILE"] ? inner : ["bundle", "exec", *inner]
    end
  end

  # CHILD ONE, and the only one that boots the real tree: Ruby's own answer to
  # "which file defines this constant", plus the scratch copy the probes run
  # against.
  module Survey
    module_function

    def run(spec_path, scratch_lib)
      require "lain"
      lib = Lain::LOADER.dirs.first
      strip(lib, scratch_lib)
      File.write(spec_path, JSON.generate({ "lib" => scratch_lib, "map" => constants(lib) }))
    end

    # `{ "Lain::Foo::BAR" => "lain/foo.rb" }`, relative so it keys against the
    # scratch tree as readily as against `lib/`. Relies on nothing but
    # `const_source_location`; a constant with no Ruby source (the 26 the
    # compiled extension defines) is absent, which is correct -- the probe
    # requires the extension by name like the real boot does.
    def constants(lib)
      walk(Lain, "Lain", Set.new([Lain]), {}, lib)
    end

    def walk(mod, cpath, seen, out, lib)
      mod.constants(false).each_with_object(out) do |cname, acc|
        here = "#{cpath}::#{cname}"
        file = mod.const_source_location(cname, false)&.first
        acc[here] = file.delete_prefix("#{lib}/") if file&.start_with?("#{lib}/")
        value = mod.const_get(cname, false)
        walk(value, here, seen, acc, lib) if value.is_a?(Module) && seen.add?(value)
      end
    end

    # `lib/` with every internal `require_relative` line gone. Ruby files are
    # copied line-filtered; everything else -- the 47MB compiled extension, the
    # nvim runtime -- is symlinked, so the copy costs milliseconds and can
    # never write back through a hard link.
    def strip(lib, dest)
      FileUtils.mkdir_p(dest)
      Dir.glob("#{lib}/**/*").each { |path| place(path, File.join(dest, path.delete_prefix("#{lib}/"))) }
    end

    def place(path, target)
      return Dir.mkdir(target) unless File.file?(path)

      return File.symlink(path, target) unless path.end_with?(".rb")

      File.write(target, File.readlines(path).reject { _1.lstrip.start_with?("require_relative ") }.join)
    end
  end

  # CHILDREN TWO AND THREE: boot the stripped tree in one load order and report
  # what did not resolve. Runs with `lib/lain.rb` never required -- it is read,
  # `eager_load` stripped off the end so the order is this script's to choose,
  # and eval'd under the scratch tree's own path so `Zeitwerk::Loader.for_gem`
  # derives that root from it rather than this script's directory.
  module Probe
    module_function

    def run(spec_path, order)
      spec = JSON.parse(File.read(spec_path))
      state = State.new(spec.fetch("lib"), spec.fetch("map"))
      Module.prepend(state.shim)
      boot(state, order)
      puts JSON.generate({ "order" => order, "hits" => state.hits, "fails" => state.fails })
    end

    def boot(state, order)
      lib = state.lib
      # Zeitwerk skips the file it is being set up FROM, and knows that file by
      # `$LOADED_FEATURES`. An eval is not a require, so it is said here.
      $LOADED_FEATURES << "#{lib}/lain.rb"
      source = File.readlines("#{lib}/lain.rb").reject { _1.start_with?("loader.eager_load") }.join
      eval(source, TOPLEVEL_BINDING, "#{lib}/lain.rb") # rubocop:disable Security/Eval
      arrive(state, order)
      state.guard("(eager_load)") { Lain::LOADER.eager_load }
    end

    # The two ways of making the tree arrive. A sorted pass requires each file
    # by PATH; the shuffled pass resolves each expected constant by NAME, which
    # is the autoload a real caller triggers -- see {Boot::ORDERS} for why the
    # third pass could not simply reuse the first's mechanism.
    def arrive(state, order)
      seed = order[/\Ashuffled:(\d+)\z/, 1]
      return files(state.lib, order).each { |file| state.guard(file) { require file } } unless seed

      cpaths(seed.to_i).each { |cpath| state.guard(cpath) { Object.const_get(cpath) } }
    end

    # Every file the loader manages, in the chosen order, minus the entry point
    # the eval above already stands for.
    def files(lib, order)
      managed = Lain::LOADER.all_expected_cpaths.keys
                            .select { _1.end_with?(".rb") && _1 != "#{lib}/lain.rb" }.sort
      order == "reverse" ? managed.reverse : managed
    end

    # Every constant path the loader expects to be able to find, shuffled --
    # sorted first so the shuffle is a function of the seed alone and not of
    # whatever order the filesystem answered in.
    def cpaths(seed)
      Lain::LOADER.all_expected_cpaths.values.sort.shuffle(random: Random.new(seed))
    end

    # The probe's whole mutable world: what has been seen, what is mid-flight,
    # and the `const_missing` that records the first. Injected into the shim
    # rather than reached through a constant, so nothing in the child depends on
    # load order to find it.
    class State
      attr_reader :lib, :hits, :fails

      def initialize(lib, map)
        @lib = lib
        @map = map.transform_values { File.join(lib, _1) }
        @hits = []
        @fails = []
        @inflight = Set.new
      end

      # Prepended, never defined: `super` has to reach Ruby's own
      # `const_missing` so an unknown name still raises its real NameError.
      def shim
        state = self
        Module.new do
          define_method(:const_missing) { |name| state.resolve(self, name) { super(name) } }
        end
      end

      # Ruby has already searched the lexical scope and the ancestors by the
      # time this runs, so the candidate walk retraces the enclosing namespaces
      # outward -- `StopReason` named inside `Lain::Agent` may mean
      # `Lain::Agent::StopReason` or `Lain::StopReason`.
      def resolve(mod, name)
        cpath = candidates(mod, name).find { @map.key?(_1) }
        return yield unless cpath && @inflight.add?(cpath)

        record(cpath)
        load_once(cpath)
        Object.const_defined?(cpath) ? Object.const_get(cpath) : yield
      end

      # In-flight for exactly the duration of the require, so a file that names
      # a constant its own load is in the middle of defining yields a real
      # NameError instead of recursing -- and so a require that RAISES does not
      # leave the name pinned, which would fake a cascade onto every later
      # reference to it.
      def load_once(cpath)
        require @map.fetch(cpath)
      ensure
        @inflight.delete(cpath)
      end

      # A `class << self` body is where this most often fires and is the one
      # place `mod.name` is nil -- a singleton class has no name, so the walk
      # would start from the empty nesting and place nothing.
      # `ErrorMiddleware`'s status table was exactly that shape, and reported
      # as UNCLASSIFIED until the attached object was asked for instead.
      def candidates(mod, name)
        parts = named_module(mod)&.name&.split("::") || []
        parts.length.downto(0).map { |depth| (parts.take(depth) + [name.to_s]).join("::") }
      end

      def named_module(mod)
        return mod if mod.name

        attached = mod.singleton_class? ? mod.attached_object : nil
        attached.is_a?(Module) && attached.name ? attached : nil
      rescue TypeError
        nil
      end

      # The innermost `lib/` frame is where the name is WRITTEN. The next frame
      # in a DIFFERENT file is what reached it at load time, and the two are
      # often not the same edit: `Mode::LayerSet` is written as a keyword
      # default in `mode.rb` and only ever evaluated because
      # `cli/command/mode.rb:53` assigns a constant from `Mode.new`. Absent
      # when the whole chain is one file -- there is nothing to add then.
      def record(cpath)
        frames = caller.select { _1.start_with?(@lib) }.map { site(_1) }
        @hits << { "const" => cpath, "from" => frames.first,
                   "at" => frames.find { file(_1) != file(frames.first) },
                   "defined_in" => @map.fetch(cpath).delete_prefix("#{@lib}/") }
      end

      def site(frame) = frame.delete_prefix("#{@lib}/").split(":in ").first

      def file(site) = site.split(":").first

      # One file's failure must not end the run, or the census reports the
      # first blocker and nothing behind it. `Exception` rather than
      # `StandardError` because `Zeitwerk::NameError` is a `NameError` but a
      # cascading `SystemStackError` is not, and both are findings.
      def guard(file)
        yield
      rescue Exception => e # rubocop:disable Lint/RescueException
        @fails << { "file" => file.delete_prefix("#{@lib}/"), "class" => e.class.name,
                    "message" => e.message.gsub("#{@lib}/", ""), "blamed" => blamed(e) }
      end

      # The file that really defines the constant a NameError names. The
      # failing SITE cannot say what a cascade is behind -- the eager load is
      # not a file -- but the missing constant can, and the survey's map is
      # already the answer. Matched on a SUFFIX because Ruby reports the name
      # as spelled (`Anthropic::Chat`), not as resolved.
      #
      # A named receiver spells the name in full, so it matches exactly or not
      # at all and this never guesses. A bare name reaches here only from an
      # ANONYMOUS module, and then the first suffix match wins -- which with
      # both `Lain::Config` and `Lain::Foo::Bar::Config` in the map answers
      # whichever the map happened to list first. No such site exists today;
      # if one ever reports here, verify its blame by hand rather than
      # trusting it, because a wrong blame landing under a flagged subtree
      # demotes a real finding to report-only and drops it from `--check`.
      def blamed(error)
        spelled = spelled_name(error)
        key = spelled && @map.keys.find { _1 == spelled || _1.end_with?("::#{spelled}") }
        key && @map.fetch(key).delete_prefix("#{@lib}/")
      end

      def spelled_name(error)
        return nil unless error.is_a?(NameError)

        owner = error.receiver
        owner.is_a?(Module) && owner.name ? "#{owner.name}::#{error.name}" : error.name.to_s
      rescue ArgumentError, NameError
        nil
      end
    end
  end

  # Turns censuses into the worklist a reader acts on. Separate from
  # {Classify} for the reason {AssertionReport} is separate from `Assertions`
  # in `bin/spec-census`: detection and formatting are different
  # responsibilities, and folding the second into the first is what pushes
  # either over `Metrics/ModuleLength`.
  module Report
    BANNER = <<~TEXT
      ZEITWERK CENSUS -- bin/zeitwerk-census

      ======================================================================
      WHAT THIS BOOTED: lib/, four times in child processes, with every
      internal `require_relative` removed -- once normally to ask Ruby which
      file defines which constant, then three times under Zeitwerk alone: the
      files required in sorted and in REVERSE sorted order, and every expected
      constant resolved by NAME in a shuffled order.

      Every entry below boots green today and is a NameError the moment a file
      is renamed past the one that happens to satisfy it. That is the whole
      point: the suite, rubocop and a reader are all silent about these.

      The shuffled pass SAMPLES the order space rather than proving anything
      over it, so a green run is evidence and not a guarantee -- which is why
      its seed is printed below and honoured from ZEITWERK_CENSUS_SEED.

      This is a WORKLIST, not a count. The number of orphan CONSTANTS in the
      tree is a different figure and a poorer gauge -- spec/zeitwerk_spec.rb
      owns that invariant and this script does not restate it.
      ======================================================================
    TEXT

    module_function

    def text(censuses, seed)
      ([BANNER, seed_line(seed), counts(censuses)] + censuses.map { |census| section(census) }).join("\n")
    end

    # Printed whether or not the shuffled pass found anything: a reader who
    # wants to try the same sample again needs it from a GREEN run too.
    #
    # It says "one sample" because a green run is not a proof and the seed
    # alone does not convey that -- the order-dependence this pass was written
    # to catch reproduced in 5 of 12 seeds, so three clean runs in a row are
    # perfectly consistent with a live defect.
    def seed_line(seed)
      "Shuffled pass seed: #{seed} (ZEITWERK_CENSUS_SEED=#{seed} replays it) -- " \
        "one sample of the order space; a clean run is not a proof\n"
    end

    def counts(censuses)
      ["Counts (found / allowlisted -- new / stale):",
       *censuses.map { |census| "  #{row(census)}" }, ""].join("\n")
    end

    def row(census)
      return "#{"#{census.shape}:".ljust(16)}#{census.found.size} -- report only" unless census.ratchet

      "#{"#{census.shape}:".ljust(16)}#{census.found.size} / #{census.known.size} " \
        "-- new #{census.new_entries.size}, stale #{census.stale.size}"
    end

    def section(census)
      ["--- #{census.title} (#{census.found.size}) ---",
       *census.found.map { |found| "  #{mark(census, found)}#{found}" },
       *census.stale.map { |name| "  STALE  #{name} -- fixed; delete its line from Known" }, ""].join("\n")
    end

    def mark(census, found) = !census.ratchet || census.known.include?(found.name) ? "       " : "NEW    "
  end

  # The entry points, and the only place this can FAIL.
  module CLI
    USAGE = <<~TEXT
      usage: bin/zeitwerk-census [--counts | --check]

        (no flag)   every finding in full, behind its banner
        --counts    the summary lines only
        --check     exit non-zero on a finding the allowlist in this script
                    does not already name

      One of the three boots arrives in a SHUFFLED order. Its seed is printed
      on every run; set ZEITWERK_CENSUS_SEED to replay one.

      Boots lib/ in child processes and takes no file arguments. Needs the gems
      and the compiled extension; run it under `bundle exec` or `direnv exec .`.
    TEXT

    MODES = { nil => :census, "--counts" => :counts, "--check" => :check }.freeze

    module_function

    # The two hidden flags are the children this script spawns of itself, and
    # they are kept out of {USAGE} because nobody types them.
    def run(argv)
      return Survey.run(argv[1], argv[2]) if argv.first == "--survey"
      return Probe.run(argv[1], argv[2]) if argv.first == "--probe"

      mode = MODES[argv.first] if argv.size <= 1
      return warn(USAGE) || exit(2) unless mode

      public_send(mode)
    end

    def census = run_with { |found, seed| puts(Report.text(found, seed)) }

    def counts = run_with { |found, seed| puts(Report.seed_line(seed), Report.counts(found)) }

    # Reports EVERY census before exiting, so a reader who fixes the first is
    # not met by a second they were never shown.
    def check
      run_with do |found, seed|
        puts(Report.seed_line(seed), found.map { |census| verdict(census) }.join("\n"))
        exit 1 if found.any?(&:over?)
      end
    end

    # One seed per invocation, chosen here and reported by every mode, so the
    # number a report prints is the number that reproduces it.
    def run_with
      seed = Boot.seed
      yield(censuses(Boot.runs(seed)), seed)
    end

    def verdict(census)
      return over(census) if census.over?
      return "ok   #{census.shape}: #{census.found.size} (report only, never ratcheted)" unless census.ratchet

      "ok   #{census.shape}: #{census.found.size} known#{stale_note(census)}"
    end

    def stale_note(census)
      census.stale.empty? ? "" : ", #{census.stale.size} stale (#{census.stale.join(", ")}) -- delete those lines"
    end

    def over(census)
      ["FAIL #{census.shape}: #{census.new_entries.size} not in the allowlist",
       *census.new_entries.map { |found| "       #{found}" },
       "     Fix it, or add its name to ZeitwerkCensus::Known in bin/zeitwerk-census,",
       "     in its own commit, saying why there. `bin/zeitwerk-census` with no flag",
       "     prints every finding with the site that names it."].join("\n")
    end

    def censuses(runs = Boot.runs)
      found = Classify.findings(runs).group_by(&:shape)
      SHAPES.map do |shape, spec|
        Census.new(shape, spec.fetch(:title), found.fetch(shape, []), spec.fetch(:known), spec.fetch(:ratchet))
      end
    end
  end
end

ZeitwerkCensus::CLI.run(ARGV) if $PROGRAM_NAME == __FILE__
